# Punch-list #26: every data/*.csv file here is git-committed on every save
# (core.storage.commit_and_push) and is, in normal use, only ever appended
# to (a new trip/lure/spot/task row) or has one existing row's cells
# rewritten in place - never restructured. Two devices saving at nearly the
# same moment (e.g. two anglers each logging a catch) both branch off the
# same commit, so a plain three-way git merge sees "a line added at the
# same position by both sides" and reports it as a content conflict, even
# though the two saves are actually unrelated and both deserve to be kept -
# confirmed empirically while building the commit_and_push() retry logic
# this file supports (see core/storage.py's docstring).
#
# `merge=union` is git's built-in driver for exactly this shape of file:
# on a conflict it keeps BOTH sides' differing lines instead of blocking,
# which is exactly "combine two independent new rows" for the common case
# this punch-list item is about. The one known tradeoff, worth remembering
# if a duplicate-looking row ever shows up: if two devices ever edit the
# very SAME existing row at the same moment (rather than each appending a
# new one), union merge doesn't conflict there either - it silently keeps
# BOTH edited versions as two rows sharing the same trip_id/item_id, rather
# than raising a conflict for a human to resolve. That's a data-quality
# gap (a duplicate to notice and delete via the existing Trip
# History/Lure Inventory/Development delete flows), not data loss - still
# strictly better than the pre-#26 behavior, where the second device's
# entire save could get silently rejected and lost. This is why
# core.storage.commit_and_push()'s retry loop still has a real
# conflict-abort path too: it's the fallback for anything NOT covered by
# this file (e.g. a binary lure photo under data/lure_images/, or
# data/dev_tasks_counter.txt).
data/*.csv merge=union
